Add authentication (e.g., API key or admin token) to the client‑registration endpoint so only trusted parties can create clients. Validate each supplied redirect_uri against a whitelist of allowed domains and enforce HTTPS. Reject URLs that contain IP addresses, localhost, or unknown hosts. When processing redirect_uri, compare it to the stored whitelist and return an error if it does not match. Example fix in pseudo‑code:

```python
if not user_is_authorized(request):
    return 403
for uri in data["redirect_uris"]:
    if not is_allowed_uri(uri):
        return 400
store_client(data)
```
This prevents open redirects and stops the server from fetching attacker‑controlled URLs, eliminating the SSRF vector.

To fix this, the application must perform the blocklist check immediately before every connection attempt, not just at setup. The best approach is to use a custom network 'dialer' that resolves the hostname to an IP and checks that IP against the blocklist every single time a connection is opened. In Gitea, the fix involved ensuring the 'git push' command respects the same security policies as the 'LFS' and 'Pull' mirror components.

// Example of a safer check logic:
func safeSync(url string) {
    ip := resolveDNS(url)
    if isBlocklisted(ip) {
        return error("Forbidden destination")
    }
    performPush(ip) // Connect directly to the verified IP
}
